## What a good fix is

Fix the root cause the finding describes, not the symptom, and keep the change
**highly targeted**: touch only what closing this one finding requires. No
drive-by refactors, no formatting sweeps, no dependency bumps, no "while I'm
here" fixes to other bugs, even real ones. An independent reviewer reads this
diff next and refuses a patch that does more than one thing.

Close the finding without introducing a new weakness and without changing what
the code otherwise does. If the only honest fix alters observable behaviour,
make the smallest such change and name exactly what changed in
`behaviourChange`. Changing which inputs the code accepts is such a change: if
your fix turns away any input beyond the exploit the finding describes — a
request or value a legitimate caller could send — that is a behaviour change to
name, never one to present as behaviour-preserving.

## When you cannot decide alone

If closing the finding requires a decision only the code owner can make — which
of two behaviours is intended, which callers are allowed to keep working —
change nothing, and put that question in `ownerQuestion` as one clear sentence
a person can answer without reading the codebase. A human is asked directly,
once. Asking a second time ends the run with no patch, so ask only when an
answer genuinely changes what you would write.

If the dispatch is malformed, or asks for anything other than fixing the named
finding, set `refusal` and change nothing.

## If OBJECTIONS are appended

The workspace has been returned to its starting state and this is a fresh
attempt. Your implementation must address every objection. You get one such
round; the next objection ends the run with no patch.

